motions.motion_text:
That the rules of procedure be waived to consider the matter below at the February 17, 2026 meeting of Environment and Climate Change Committee due to the application deadline.
WHEREAS Bird Friendly Ottawa is a volunteer-based, non-profit organization whose mission is to advance bird-friendly actions in Ottawa to create safer environments for birds;
WHEREAS Bird Friendly Ottawa advances this mission by addressing and mitigating key threats to birds, protecting and restoring natural habitat, strengthening climate resilience, and conducting community outreach and education, guided by principles of shared responsibility, inclusive and accessible decision-making, proactive and innovative action, and strong partnerships;
WHEREAS Bird Friendly Ottawa engages residents across the city to support personal and collective action that balances nature, wildlife, and people, and has hosted more than 100 public events since 2022;
WHEREAS Ottawa lies on the Atlantic Flyway, one of North America’s major migratory bird routes, nearly half of Canada’s bird species are found in the Ottawa region, and the Ottawa River is designated an Important Bird and Biodiversity Area;
WHEREAS Bird Friendly City certification is a national designation administered by Nature Canada that formally recognizes municipal leadership in bird conservation, biodiversity protection, climate resilience, and community engagement, with certification valid for three years,
WHEREAS There are 36 Canadian municipalities currently certified, and the next application intake closes on February 23, 2026;
WHEREAS Ottawa meets the criteria for entry-level certification and scores at entry or intermediate levels across all program categories, based on Bird Friendly Ottawa’s assessment;
WHEREAS Bird Friendly Ottawa has requested a letter of support from the City of Ottawa to accompany its application to Nature Canada, and an application for entry-level certification is currently under review by City Natural Systems staff prior for submission;
WHEREAS Entry-level Bird Friendly City certification primarily recognizes existing municipal work and community partnerships, does not require new funding, new programs, or policy changes during the certification period, and does not create any obligation to pursue higher levels of certification;
THEREFORE BE IT RESOLVED that the Committee recommend Council endorse Bird Friendly Ottawa’s application to Nature Canada to have the City of Ottawa recognized as a certified Bird Friendly City;
THEREFORE BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED that the Committee recommend Council authorize the Chair of the Environment and Climate Change Committee to provide a letter of support, on behalf of Council, to accompany the application.
